{{also|Dissident}}
==English==

===Etymology===
{{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|dissidens}}, ''[[-entis]]'', present participle of {{m|la|dissidere||to sit apart, to disagree}}; ''[[dis-]]'' + ''[[sedere]]'' to sit.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈdɪsɪdənt/|lang=en}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|-}}

# In a manner that [[disagree]]s; [[dissent]]ing; discordant; different.
#* {{quote-book|author={{w|Ralph Robinson (humanist)|Ralph Robinson}}|title=Utopia|type=translation|by={{w|Thomas More}}|passage=And yet ye most parte of them is more '''dissident''' from the maners of the worlde nowe a dayes, then my communication was.|page=66|year=1551}}
#* {{quote-journal|journal=Journal of the Royal Society of Arts|volume=25|page=670|date=25 May 1877|passage=On flattening either the higher or lower reed separately, by partially pushing in either one of the pulls, '''dissident''' beats instantly arose, which would be made to disappear by partially pushing in the other reed.|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=ouBFAQAAMAAJ&dq=%22dissident%22&pg=PA670#v=onepage&q=%22dissident%22&f=false}}
#* {{quote-book|title=The Convergence of Corporate Governance: Promise and Prospects|author=A. Rasheed|year=2012|passage=In this chapter we look at the question of convergence in corporate governance by evaluating '''dissident''' proxy proposals in Canada.|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=aLilBAEQDbQC&lpg=PT40&dq=%22dissident%22&pg=PT40#v=onepage&q=%22dissident%22&f=false}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A person who formally opposes the current political structure, opposes the political group in power, opposes the policies of the political group in power, or opposes current laws.
#* {{quote-magazine|magazine=The Speaker|date=15 June 1895|page=658|passage=I once more find myself a '''dissident''', and a '''dissident''' in a very small minority.|title=Claude Monet|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=lhM-AQAAMAAJ&dq=%22a%20dissident%22&pg=PA658#v=onepage&q=%22a%20dissident%22&f=false|volume=11}}
#* {{quote-book|title=The Voices of Glasnost|page=280|editor=Stephen F. Cohen|year=1990|passage=It is largely the story of a man who fell from being a potential leader of the Soviet Communist Party in the early 1960s to being an outcast by the mid-1970s — a '''dissident''' in the eyes of officialdom, a "half-dissident" in his own eyes.|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=1KKAbbo3Rg8C&lpg=PA280&dq=%22a%20dissident%22&pg=PA280#v=onepage&q=%22a%20dissident%22&f=false|chapter=The Autobiography of a "Half-Dissident"|author=Len Karpinsky}}
#* {{quote-book|title=Divided We Stand: The Strategy and Psychology of Ireland's Dissident Terrorists|passage=Before Frampton published his book ''Legion of the Rearguard'', an exhaustive examination of the '''dissidents''', he highlighted sections of it released in a report by the International Center for the Study of Radicalisation (ICSR) at King's College, London, entitled "The Return of the Militants."|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=7Bf-9w2kvKgC&lpg=PT26&dq=%22dissidents%22&pg=PT26#v=onepage&q=%22dissidents%22&f=false|year=2013}}
# {{lb|en|ecclesiastical}} One who disagrees or dissents; one who separates from the established religion.
# {{lb|en|historical|sometimes capitalized}} In the kingdom of {{l|en|Poland}}, the name for Christians not part of the Roman Catholic Church.
#* {{quote-magazine|magazine=The Monthly Intelligencer|date=May 1733|issue=24|passage=The Day of Election was fixt for the 25th of ''Aug''. But a Protest was enter'd by the '''''Dissidents''''', who had been excluded.|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=yk87AAAAYAAJ&lpg=PA272&ots=VpcYuifD3m&dq=Prince%20of%20Anhalt%20with%20the%20Princess%20Albertina&pg=PA272#v=onepage&q=Prince%20of%20Anhalt%20with%20the%20Princess%20Albertina&f=false|page=272}}
#* {{quote-book|title=Reflections on the Affairs of the Dissidents in Poland|page=7|year=1767|passage=The Article which enjoins Peace among the '''Dissidents''' was signed by all the Catholics then present, and so are also the continual Repetitions of it in all the Pacta Conventa, and in the fame Terms, to the Death of the late King; whereas the Confederacies of 1717 and 1733 are not signed by the '''Dissidents''', who were expelled from thence by Force.|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=BMIvAAAAYAAJ&dq=%22dissidents%22&pg=PA7#v=onepage&q=%22dissidents%22&f=false}}
#* {{quote-magazine|magazine=The Critical Review: Or, Annals of Literature|volume=25|passage=These Pieces are introduced by a very sensible preface, explaining the hardships and injustice which have been inflicted upon the '''Dissidents''' of Poland.|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=I8EPAAAAQAAJ&dq=%22dissidents%22&pg=PA206#v=onepage&q=%22dissidents%22&f=false|title=Original Pieces, concerning the present Situation of the Protestants and Greeks in Poland|year=1768}}
#* {{quote-book|title=The nationalities of Europe|volume=1|author=Robert Gordon Latham|passage=A Socinian was a '''Dissident''', and a member of the Greek Church was a '''Dissident'''; and these '''Dissidents''' agreed to act together. Even a liberal Romanist might be called a '''Dissident'''.|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=20QBAAAAQAAJ&dq=%22a%20dissident%22&pg=PA53#v=onepage&q=%22a%20dissident%22&f=false|year=1893}}
#* {{quote-book|title=Europe: The Struggle for Supremacy, from 1453 to the Present|author=Brendan Simms|passage=The rest, about half a million Russian Orthodox and about the same number of Protestants, were known as ''''dissidents''''; the huge Jewish community defied classification.|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=XiHU2R59Vl8C&lpg=PT83&dq=%22dissidents%22%20%22poland%22%20%22christians%22&pg=PT83#v=onepage&q=%22dissidents%22%20%22poland%22%20%22christians%22&f=false|year=2013}}

